@jayyuen66/dsh-scrapling README · <img src="https://api.iconify.design/lucide/shield.svg?color=%23658EFF&height=22" height="22" width="22" align="absmiddle" alt="" /> What it does：- **Browser rendering** — JavaScript execution via patchright.
- **Structured extraction** — CSS/XPath selection returning data, not a wall of Markdown.
- **Self-healing selectors** — Scrapling's `adaptive` mode relocates an element after the site's markup changes; a selector that returns `None` after a class rename returns the relocated element with `adaptive` on.

@jayyuen66/dsh-scrapling README · <img src="https://api.iconify.design/lucide/package.svg?color=%23658EFF&height=22" height="22" width="22" align="absmiddle" alt="" /> Install：- **Requires Node.js `>= 24.9`.** This is an ESM-only package, and the build entry points (`build-client.ts`, `build-host.ts`, `build-cli.ts`) are executed by `node` directly, so the floor has to sit where TypeScript type stripping is on by default (Node 22.18 up). The number itself is the host floor: `0.2.2` hosts declare Node `>= 24.9.0`, and a host below it never starts.
- **Requires host `dsh` at the `0.2.1-rc.5` or `0.2.2-alpha.3` line.** The requirement the host actually reads is `engines.dsh` (`^0.2.1-rc.5 || ^0.2.2-alpha.3`), and it decides alone; a host on a later line is refused and needs the explicit risk grant `dsh plugin --profile <p> allow-version <pkg>@<ver> --dsh-version <exact> --accept-risk`. The support window rolls: this package is verified against every released host version inside the window at publish time and re-verified once the window moves, so the version number in this bullet is not a standing promise.
- **Requires Python 3.14+ with Scrapling installed** — named by `pythonBin`, or

@jayyuen66/dsh-scrapling README · <img src="https://api.iconify.design/lucide/package.svg?color=%23658EFF&height=22" height="22" width="22" align="absmiddle" alt="" /> Install：- **Why `rag` and not `fetchers`:** `rag` is `fetchers` plus `markdownify`, and the default `extractionType` is `markdown` — with plain `fetchers` the first fetch raises `ModuleNotFoundError` at the Markdown conversion.
- **Version range:** developed and verified against Scrapling **0.5**, through interfaces that are not stable API surface (`Selector`, `Spider`, `SessionManager`, and the converter's noise-stripping helpers). The upper bound is pinned below `0.6`; outside the range the guards still run, but the anti-injection cleaning and the crawl path may not behave as documented. In 0.5 the browser backend is patchright only (the old standalone stealth fetch is gone; its anti-detection arguments live on `scrapling_render`), `capture_xhr` became session-level recording (hence `scrapling_session_network`), and `extra_flags` became additive — this package sends none, and WebRTC leak protection is carried by the `blockWebrtc` switch.
- **Without the installer, the browser engine is yours.** The optional CLI can

@jayyuen66/dsh-scrapling README · <img src="https://api.iconify.design/lucide/circle-slash.svg?color=%23658EFF&height=22" height="22" width="22" align="absmiddle" alt="" /> What it does not provide：- **It is not a search engine.** Discovery runs in the Python sidecar against one of six backends (`searchBackend`: `auto` / `tavily` / `bing` / `brave` / `serper` / `duckduckgo` / `searxng`); Scrapling's own `google_search` option only sets a referer header. Each backend needs its own setting (`tavilyApiKey`, `braveApiKey`, `serperApiKey`, `searxngEndpoint`) — a backend that is selected but not configured reports `SEARCH_AUTH` instead of silently switching to another one.
- **The npm tarball bundles no interpreter or browser.** They come from your machine or from the optional CLI installer, which downloads the verified macOS arm64 set into `dataDir/runtime`; missing pieces remove the matching tools instead of being fetched at load time.
- **It adds no HTTP route on the host side.** The settings card is a host config form and this package exposes no `/api` surface. The one socket that really listens is the MCP server under `integrationMode: mcp`, bound to `127.0.0.1` (`http://127.0.0.1:<

@jayyuen66/dsh-scrapling README · <img src="https://api.iconify.design/lucide/globe.svg?color=%23658EFF&height=22" height="22" width="22" align="absmiddle" alt="" /> The tools：- **Quotas.** At most 8 open sessions (3 of them browser sessions, each a real Chromium process), 2 crawls in flight, per-page bodies clipped by `*MaxOutputChars`, 8 MB per protocol frame, 16 queued frames. A ceiling answers with a specific code (`SESSION_LIMIT` / `CRAWL_BUSY` / `BAD_FRAME`) the model can back off from.
- **`scrapling_crawl` returns a job id immediately** — a crawl takes minutes and a tool call has a timeout. Watch it with `job_list`, read it with `job_output`, stop it with `job_kill`; the tool needs a jobs registry that can attach a controller, and a profile without one simply does not register it (single-page fetching is unaffected). The crawl has a wall-clock ceiling (`crawlMaxRuntimeSeconds`) and never leaves the start domain. Politeness defaults follow Scrapling's spider defaults — robots.txt is **not** obeyed, per-domain delay is 0, up to 4 requests run in parallel — and is a deployment decision, not a per-call one (see [Settings](#settings)).
- **`scrapling_answer` fetches the page fir

@jayyuen66/dsh-scrapling README · <img src="https://api.iconify.design/lucide/arrow-right-left.svg?color=%23658EFF&height=22" height="22" width="22" align="absmiddle" alt="" /> Replacing the built-in fetch and search：1. leave `provideWebFetch` / `provideWebSearch` at their default (`true`);
2. set `fetchProvider` / `searchProvider` to `"scrapling"` in the **deployment** config.

A configured id wins over auto-selection, which is why step 2 is required — and with two usable providers and no id named, `ctx.web` answers `WEB_PROVIDER_AMBIGUOUS` and the tool becomes unavailable outright. Two consequences of pointing that line at this plugin:

@jayyuen66/dsh-scrapling README · <img src="https://api.iconify.design/lucide/arrow-right-left.svg?color=%23658EFF&height=22" height="22" width="22" align="absmiddle" alt="" /> Replacing the built-in fetch and search：- **If the sidecar cannot start, the built-in `web_fetch` reports `WEB_PROVIDER_CONFIGURED_UNAVAILABLE`** rather than quietly falling back to plain HTTP.
- **If you later remove the plugin but leave the line in**, the built-in tools report `WEB_PROVIDER_CONFIGURED_MISSING` — go back and remove `fetchProvider` / `searchProvider` too.
- **The fetch provider really applies the output budget**: a body over `fetchMaxOutputChars` is cut to a head/tail window and flagged `truncated`, not passed through with a flag. Failures keep the sidecar code prefix (`URL_BLOCKED`, `SIDECAR_TIMEOUT`, `SIDECAR_UNAVAILABLE`), so a policy refusal is distinguishable from a crash or a timeout.

@jayyuen66/dsh-scrapling README · <img src="https://api.iconify.design/lucide/plug-zap.svg?color=%23658EFF&height=22" height="22" width="22" align="absmiddle" alt="" /> MCP mode：- **Endpoint** `http://127.0.0.1:<mcpPort>/mcp` (`mcpPort`, default `39217`). Only loopback is bound; a request whose declared body exceeds 1 MiB is refused with `413`, and any other path gets `404`. The server is stateless: each request gets its own protocol instance, so nothing survives across them.
- **Auth** off by default. Set the environment variable `DSH_SCRAPLING_MCP_TOKEN` and every request must then carry `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. The token is only ever read from the environment, never from the profile, so it does not land in a config file.
- **Tools** are the same `ToolDefinition` objects native registers: same JSON Schema, same argument validation, same renderers. `scrapling_answer` is not served — it routes through the model of the hosting session, which an external client does not have. `search` exists only on this surface, because an MCP client has no `ctx.web` seam. The gates are identical: with `allowBrowserActions=false` there is no `session_act`, with `allowFileDownloads=false` the

@jayyuen66/dsh-scrapling README · <img src="https://api.iconify.design/lucide/plug-zap.svg?color=%23658EFF&height=22" height="22" width="22" align="absmiddle" alt="" /> MCP mode：Connecting dsh needs `@deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-client`, which this plugin deliberately does not depend on. Pin the build that matches the current host — npm `latest` is older than the host's own line and the host rejects the mismatch:

```bash
dsh plugin --profile <name> add @deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-client@0.2.2-rc.1

@jayyuen66/dsh-scrapling README · <img src="https://api.iconify.design/lucide/network.svg?color=%23658EFF&height=22" height="22" width="22" align="absmiddle" alt="" /> Proxies：- **The resolved proxy is handed to the browser engine explicitly.** Chromium does not read `HTTP_PROXY` / `ALL_PROXY`, so a proxy that is only exported in a shell would cover static fetches but not the browser — this package closes that gap by passing the resolved proxy on every browser fetch. A **browser session** still picks its proxy once, at `scrapling_session_open`: switching it means a new browser context, which throws away the session's cookies and login state — precisely the reason a session exists. Static sessions decide per request.
- **PAC is the one shape that needs a hand-filled `proxyUrl`.** Evaluating a PAC script means downloading it and running JavaScript, and the dialects differ per platform, so this package does not guess; static fetching says so on stderr, and the browser follows PAC natively through its own system settings.
- **A proxy and synthetic DNS are two different problems.** A fake-IP proxy resolves **every** domain into `198.18.0.0/15` (TUN clients often `100.64.0.0/10`); neithe

@jayyuen66/dsh-scrapling README · <img src="https://api.iconify.design/lucide/lock.svg?color=%23658EFF&height=22" height="22" width="22" align="absmiddle" alt="" /> Data and privacy：| Aspect | Content |
| --- | --- |
| On disk | Under the sidecar working directory (`dataDir`, default `~/.dsh-scrapling`): Scrapling's `adaptive` fingerprint database when `adaptiveStorage: sqlite` (the default), `scrapling_download`'s output in `downloads/`, the managed Python and Chromium installed by `setup --feature … --apply` (`runtime/`), and the MCP entry written by `setup --mode … --apply` (`mcp/<mode>.patch.yml`). With `adaptiveStorage: redis` the fingerprints are not written under `dataDir` at all — they go to the Redis at `redisUrl`, keyed `<prefix>:<registrable-domain>:<sha256(identifier)>`; that URL defaults to loopback, and pointing it at another machine hands this data to that machine. The crawl queue lands only when `crawlCheckpointDir` is set (the upstream `Collector(crawldir=…)` writes it); an empty value writes nothing. Login-state files go under `sessionStateDir` when it is configured. Screenshots are handed to the host attachment service, which decides where they live. Settings val

@jayyuen66/dsh-scrapling README · <img src="https://api.iconify.design/lucide/wrench.svg?color=%23658EFF&height=22" height="22" width="22" align="absmiddle" alt="" /> Development：- **The Python half is gated like the TypeScript half.** `check` ends with `check:py`: an interpreter report, a face count (the shipped `.py` files and the test directory must both be there — a missing test directory makes "clean" and "untested" indistinguishable), a dependency consistency check, ruff check, ruff format, mypy, the sidecar end-to-end, bandit (all severities; `# nosec` only for justified false positives), and a coverage floor measured **only over the modules that ship**. Dev tools: `pip install -r py/requirements-dev.txt` into the interpreter `test:py` uses.
- **The Python 3.14 floor is one fact in four places:** both READMEs, the ruff and mypy targets, and a runtime gate in `py/bridge.py` that exits before any Scrapling import with a clean stdout — stdout is protocol frames, so an old interpreter shows the exit code and a stderr line instead of garbage.
